<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §6 Bidirectional Attention -->
<section id="s-attn" data-code="attention">
<div class="section-no">06 — Bidirectional Attention</div>
<h2>Why the assistant uses bidirectional masks</h2>
<p>
  Standard causal LM attention masks ensure each position can only attend to positions at or before it (lower-triangular mask). But the assistant does not need to be causal — it sees the entire draft sequence at once and uses the backbone's KV as context. Bidirectional attention lets it refine token embeddings with both left and right context.
</p>
<p>
  There's a subtle implementation detail for sliding-window attention (SWA): the backbone's SWA uses a <em>past-looking</em> window, but the assistant's bidirectional mask is created with a <em>future-looking</em> perspective by default. The code compensates by <strong>flipping the SWA mask on the KV axis</strong>.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">attention-masks.svg</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="attn-wrap">
      <div class="attn-col causal-col">
        <h4>Causal (backbone)</h4>
        <div class="attn-grid" id="causal-grid"></div>
        <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;text-align:center">Each token sees only past tokens.<br>Standard autoregressive mask.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="attn-col bidir-col">
        <h4>Bidirectional (assistant)</h4>
        <div class="attn-grid" id="bidir-grid"></div>
        <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;text-align:center">All tokens attend to all others.<br>Backbone KV provides causal context.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §7 Full Loop -->
<section id="s-loop" data-code="full-loop">
<div class="section-no">07 — The Full Loop</div>
<h2>Putting it all together</h2>
<p>
  At generation time, HuggingFace's <code>model.generate()</code> orchestrates the speculative decoding loop automatically when <code>assistant_model</code> is provided. The backbone and assistant alternate: assistant drafts a batch, backbone verifies, accepted tokens are committed, and the loop continues.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">generation-loop.svg — Accept / Reject</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Auto-playing</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ar-demo" id="ar-demo">
      <div class="ar-row">
        <span class="ar-label">Draft</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="at0"> is</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="at1"> Paris</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="at2">,</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="at3"> the</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="at4"> most</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ar-row">
        <span class="ar-label">Verify</span>
        <span class="ar-tok pending" id="vt0"> is</span>
        <span class="ar-tok pending" id="vt1"> Paris</span>
        <span class="ar-tok pending" id="vt2">,</span>
        <span class="ar-tok pending" id="vt3"> the</span>
        <span class="ar-tok pending" id="vt4"> most</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ar-row" style="margin-top:.25rem">
        <span class="ar-label" style="color:var(--green)">Accept</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="acc0" style="opacity:0"> is <span class="badge"></span></span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="acc1" style="opacity:0"> Paris <span class="badge"></span></span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="acc2" style="opacity:0">, <span class="badge"></span></span>
      </div>
      <div class="ar-row">
        <span class="ar-label" style="color:var(--red)">Reject</span>
        <span class="ar-tok" id="rej3" style="opacity:0"> the <span class="badge"></span></span>
        <span class="ar-tok" style="opacity:0;font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted)" id="rej-note">(backbone corrects from here)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  After the first rejected token, all subsequent draft tokens are discarded and the backbone generates the correct continuation. In this example, 3 tokens are accepted and 1 is rejected — still a net 3× gain over pure autoregressive generation.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">🚀</span>
  <span>Use it with: <code>model.generate(inputs, assistant_model=assistant, max_new_tokens=200)</code>. No other changes required — HuggingFace handles the entire speculative decoding loop.</span>
</div>
</section>

